{:edb/db-before-t 1596 :edb/db-after-t 1597 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534909 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:43:43.812Z" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147158 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2$." 13194139534909 false] [17592186147158 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = 2.$" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147158 1145 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }2{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq 0$ or $s = 0.$\n\n- If $s \\neq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-st}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{s}{e}^{-st})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>0, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(0) & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{-0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & =2{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\n- For $s > 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n\n- For $s < 0,$ we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =-\\frac{2}{s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{-sa}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{s}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $f(t)=2$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s},$ defined for $s > 0.$" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147158 1145 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nF(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}2e^{-st}dt \\\\\n&= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq 0\n$$\n\nor $s = 0$.\n\n- If $s \\neq 0$, we have\n$F(s)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-st}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(-\\frac{1}{s}e^{-st}) \\mid _{0}^{a}; =-\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1)$.\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > 0, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < 0$.\n- If $s = 0$, we have\n$F(0)|= 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{-0t}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}dt; = 2lim_(a → ∞)t \\mid _{0}^{a}; = 2lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = 2lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s = 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n- For $s > 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is convergent\n- For $s < 0$, we have that $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} 2e^{-st} \\textrm{d} t$ is divergent\n\nEvaluating our integral for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= -\\frac{2}{s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{s}(0 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=2\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{2}{s}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > 0$." 13194139534909 false] [17592186147159 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}$." 13194139534909 false] [17592186147159 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{-2t}.$" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147159 1145 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[0,\\infty),$ the Laplace transform of $f,$ denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s),$ is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \n\\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite. \n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{-2t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st-2t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether $s \\neq -2$ or $s = -2.$\n\n- If $s \\neq -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{-2-s}{e}^{(-2-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{-2-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a}={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & s>-2, \\\\ \\infty ,\\, & s<-2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $s = -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-2) & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(-2-(-2))t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{0t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }t\\,{|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(a-0) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }a \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(-2-s)a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}-\\frac{1}{s+2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of $f(t)=e^{-2t}$ is $F(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2},$ defined for $s > -2.$" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147159 1145 "For a function $f(t)$ defined on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ the Laplace transform of $f$, denoted $\\mathcal L\\{f(t)\\}$ or $F(s)$, is a function of the variable $s$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\} = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nprovided that this integral exists and is finite.\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st} \\cdot e^{-2t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st - 2t}dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo evaluate the last integral, we must consider whether\n\n$$\ns \\neq -2\n$$\n\nor $s = -2$.\n\n- If $s \\neq -2$, we have\n$F(s)|= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - s)t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{-2 - s}e^{(-2 - s)t}) \\mid _{0}^{a}; = \\frac{1}{-2 - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} - 1); = \\frac{1}{s + 2}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}); = \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a}$\nLet's evaluate the limit. We have\n$lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}s > - 2, \\\\ ∞,\\end{vmatrix}s < - 2$.\n- If $s = -2$, we have\n$F(-2)|= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(-2 - (-2))t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{0t}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}dt; = lim_(a → ∞)t \\mid _{0}^{a}; = lim_(a → ∞)(a - 0); = lim_(a → ∞)a; = ∞$.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- For $s \\leq -2$ the integral is divergent.\n- For $s > -2$ the integral is convergent.\n\nEvaluating for $s > -2$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot lim_(a → ∞)e^{(-2 - s)a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2} - \\frac{1}{s + 2} \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s + 2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Laplace transform of\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{-2t}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s+2}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s > -2$." 13194139534909 false] [17592186147160 1100 "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{2\\textrm i t}$, where $t, s \\in\\mathbb R$?" 13194139534909 false] [17592186147160 1100 "What is the Laplace transform of $f(t) = e^{2\\textrm i t},$ where $t, s \\in\\mathbb  R?$" 13194139534909 true] [17592186147160 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f(t)} &= F(s) = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}f(t)dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{-st}e^{2\\textrm{i}t}dt = ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= ∫_{0}^{∞}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)∫_{0}^{a}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}dt \\\\\n&= lim_(a → ∞)(\\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)t}) \\mid _{0}^{a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\textrm{i} - s}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a} - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(1 - lim_(a → ∞)e^{(2\\textrm{i} - s)a}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}(lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot e^{2\\textrm{i}a})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall Euler's formula:\n\n$$\ne^{\\textrm i\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + \\textrm i \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nF(s)= \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i} - \\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\\left[\\lim_{a\\to \\infty}e^{-sa}\\cdot \\left(\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)\\right) \\right]\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following cases:\n\n- For $s < 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ increases without bound while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s < 0$.\n- For $s = 0$, we have simply $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$, which oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit does not exist for $s = 0$.\n- For $s > 0$, the term $e^{-sa}$ decays to zero while $\\cos(2a) + \\textrm i\\sin(2a)$ oscillates rapidly as $a\\to\\infty$. Therefore, the limit of their product equals zero.\n\nTherefore, for $s > 0$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(s) &= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}[lim_(a → ∞)e^{-sa} \\cdot (\\cos (2a) + \\textrm{i}\\sin (2a))] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{s - 2\\textrm{i}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Laplace transform of $e^{2\\textrm i t}$ is\n\n$$\nF(s) =\\dfrac{1}{s-2\\textrm i}\n$$\n\ndefined for $s>0$." 13194139534909 false] [17592186147160 1145 "We apply the definition of the Laplace transform:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the function $f(t)=e^{2\\textrm i t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}{e}^{2\\text{i}t}\\text{d}t={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating in the usual way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(s) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t}\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)t})|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\text{i}-s}({lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s-2\\text{i}}(1-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{e}^{(2\\text{i}-s)a}) \\\\  & =